# Explain: the design rationale of a given solution

You explain a solution to a problem in terms of a design space: which parts of it the requirements call for, what
supports them, and which choices the requirements force and which the designers made freely. You write the
explanation, with every point you evaluated on the way, as one rationale file that `ddspace rationale check`
accepts. The work is a loop: form a hypothesis about why part of the solution is there, evaluate it, record what
you observed, and let the observation decide what to test next.

## The input

The user gives the problem with the request: a file to read (such as `PROBLEM.md`) or text written in the request
itself. A problem has a context and requirements: functional ones, quality attributes (best as scenarios) and
constraints. When the requirements are not labelled, take them from the text and say how you read them. The solution to explain comes the same way: a file (such as `SOLUTION.md`) or text that describes the system as built. When
the request gives neither a file nor text, look for `PROBLEM.md` and `SOLUTION.md` in the working folder; when there is none, ask
for the problem before you start.

## The design space

- A **dimension** is a design concern the solution must settle: where the data lives, how replicas agree.
- A **value** is one option for a dimension: local disks, a log in object storage. The values of a dimension are
  alternatives to each other.
- A **relation** links two dimensions: one requires, constrains or excludes the other.
- A **design point** is a set of values, at most one per dimension. The solution is one design point.
- A **region** is a partial design point: some of the solution's values. Every design with those values falls in
  the region. A region **justifies** the solution when the requirements call for its values and what is known
  supports that combination.
- A **contrast** is a region with one value changed to another value of the same dimension. If the change breaks
  a requirement or loses the support, the requirements **force** the solution's value; if not, the designers chose
  it **freely**.
- **Evaluating** a point or region means judging it as a whole: for each requirement it bears on, does it `meet`
  it, meet it `partly`, or `miss` it; which of its values conflict; and what supports the combination.

**Is there a design space?** A design space is a `ddspace` workspace: a folder with a `ddspace.yaml` and a wiki of
dimensions, values and relations, which the `ddspace` command reads. Look for `references/design-space.md` next to
this file, in the folder this skill was loaded from (in a project, `.agents/skills/ddspace-explain/` or
`.claude/skills/ddspace-explain/`; use `ls` or `cat`: file searches such as `rg --files` skip hidden folders). If it
is there, run the command it gives for what the space is, exactly as it writes it, in the working folder. A design space exists only when that command prints one: then read the reference before
step 1. It says which `ddspace` command does each operation below (list dimensions and values, find evidence, evaluate a point), how to write ids, what to do with decisions the space does not cover, and the `space` part of the file. When the reference is missing, `ddspace`
is not installed, or the command finds no space, there is no space: you do every operation yourself, from the
problem and what you know, and the support for a point is your own knowledge, which you say. When `ddspace` runs
but finds no space, tell the user so in one line before you start ("`ddspace` is installed, but this folder has
no design space: I continue without one") and go on without a space.

**Without a space** you work zero-shot: you do not build or write a space, you name each dimension and value
from the solution, the problem and your own knowledge when you need it. The file's `space` is `null`, a
dimension's id is its name (`Storage`) and a value's id is `Dimension: Value` (`Storage: Local disks`). Write a
name the same way every time: the check reads the dimensions and values from these names.

## Budget

Evaluate at most **{{ default_points }} points or regions** (the `budget`), unless the user names another number; never
more than {{ max_points }}. Each one is a step of the `trajectory`. Stop as soon as the solution is explained: a simple
solution may need only two or three steps.

## The list of design points

Record every design point you evaluate once, in `points`: an id (`P1`, `P2`, …), its `values`, a short
`description` and what supports it (`attested_by`). A step names its point by id (`"point": "P1"`). Before you
evaluate a point, look it up in the list: when a point with the same values is already there, the step names that
id and you do not evaluate or describe it again. If a description must change (rarely), change it in the list
only: every step that names the point shows the new one. Without a space, describe each new point with
`ddspace point --no-space --domain "<problem title>" "<Dimension: Value>" ...` (one call per point, the values
named as in the file) and record the `description` it prints; `attested_by` stays empty. If that call fails, or
`ddspace` is not installed, keep the description `null`, never one of your own. Every description ends with the point's own trade-off
(`Trade-off: …`): what the combination of values gains and what it gives up, whatever the requirements.

## Gaps in the space

With a space, use it as it is. Never add, rename or remove a dimension, value or relation to make the solution
fit. Where the space falls short, record the gap instead of forcing a fit:

- **A decision the space has no dimension or value for** (with a wiki): list it in `uncovered` (the reference
  says how).
- **A requirement** no dimension bears on (`no_dimension`, with the `nearest_dimension_id` or `null`), or one the
  solution does not address (`not_in_solution`), or gives up for a stronger one (`traded_off`): list it in
  `unaddressed` with that reason and a `note`.
- **A value that only roughly matches** the solution's decision: keep the value, and mark the choice and the
  ADR option `"fit": "approximate"` with a `fit_note` saying what the value leaves out or stretches.

Without a space there is nothing to fall short of: `uncovered` stays empty, no requirement is `no_dimension`, no
fit is approximate, and `nearest_dimension_id` stays `null`. In both cases, every requirement is either a driver
of some ADR or listed in `unaddressed` (`not_in_solution` and `traded_off` apply with a space or without), never
both. Write `"unaddressed": []` when every requirement is addressed.

## Steps

1. **Requirements.** Read the problem and give each requirement an id: `R1`, `R2`, … for functional
   requirements, `Q1`, … for quality requirements (with the quality `attribute`, e.g. `durability`), `C1`, … for
   constraints. Keep the problem's own wording in `text`. Choose a short file id (lowercase, hyphens), `<id>`.
2. **The solution's decisions.** Read the solution and list each design decision it makes. For each, copy the
   sentence or phrase of the solution that states it, word for word: that is its `solution_quote`.
3. **Map the solution onto the space.** For each decision, find its dimension and the value the quote describes.
   Without a space, name them yourself: the dimension the decision settles and the solution's value for it. The
   other values a contrast needs, you name when you contrast.
4. **The solution as a point.** Evaluate the solution's values together (`move: "solution"`, no `parent`): this
   is step 1.
5. **Hypotheses.** Group the solution's values by the requirements that call for them: each group is a candidate
   region, and the hypothesis is that those requirements explain it. Order the groups by how constraining their
   requirements are.
6. **Evaluate, one step at a time,** starting with the first group. For every step:
   1. **Hypothesis.** Say which requirements you expect to explain the values, in `hypothesis`.
   2. **Evaluate** it, or reuse it from `points` when the list already has it: for each requirement it bears on,
      `meets`, `partly` or `misses`, in `assessment`; and what supports the combination. Record a new point in
      `points`.
   3. **Trade-offs.** A trade-off is a choice that serves some requirements at the expense of others. Start
      from the description's `Trade-off:`, which says what the values give up, and say which requirements that
      touches. In `tradeoffs`, record one entry per cause: the `values` of the point that cause it, the few
      requirements it `gains` (judged `meets` or `partly`) and the few it `costs` (judged `partly` or `misses`,
      never `meets`), and a `note` on how those values pull the requirements apart. Do not restate the
      assessment: a trade-off over every requirement says nothing the assessment does not. A requirement that is
      only unproven (no number to check a target against) is not a cost: say so in `observation`. A point that
      meets some requirements and not others needs at least one trade-off; one that meets every requirement it is
      judged on has `[]`.
   4. **Observe.** Write what the evaluation showed in `observation`, including anything you did not expect.
   5. **Verdict:** `grounded` (the requirements call for these values and what is known supports them),
      `partial` (only some of that holds) or `ungrounded`.
   5. **Choose the next step:**
      - a `region` (`parent`: the step it narrows, at first the solution step) for the next group, or a smaller
        region when a region is `partial` and you suspect one value does not belong to it;
      - a `contrast` (`parent`: the region) when a region is grounded but you cannot tell whether a requirement
        forces one of its values: change that value to another value of its dimension, evaluate, and say in
        `observation` whether the value is forced or free. Contrast the values whose reason is least clear
        first; a value no requirement bears on is free without a contrast.
7. **Stop** when grounded regions cover the solution's main values and each of their values is known to be
   forced or free, or when the budget is spent.
8. **The regions.** Name the one to three regions that best justify the solution: grounded, covering the most of
   its values, and overlapping little. Each names the step that evaluated it, its values (a subset of the
   solution's), the requirements that call for it (`drivers`) and a `reason`.
9. **The solution as one alternative.** Write one choice per mapped decision, with its `solution_quote`, the
   requirement ids it serves (`drivers`), `forced` (`true`, `false`, or `null` when no step settled it) and a
   `reason`. Its `step` is the solution step. Put in `considered` only the values the solution text says the
   designers weighed.
10. **ADRs.** Describe the solution as architecture decision records (`adrs`). Group its values by the
   requirements behind them (the regions are a good start) and write one ADR per group: its `drivers`, a `title`,
   the `context` (what the drivers demand) and its `options`, each a partial design point (`values`) with its
   `pros`, its `cons`, a `reason` and the `step` that evaluated it when one did. The solution's values for the
   group are the one `chosen` option (`in: ["S"]`; say in `reason` whether the requirements force it). The
   contrasts you evaluated and the options the solution says the designers weighed are `discarded` options.
   Every value of the solution belongs to the chosen option of some ADR. Write the ADRs for a reader who
   has not seen the trajectory: their text never refers to steps (the `step` field links them). List every
   requirement no ADR addresses in `unaddressed`, with its reason (see "Gaps in the space").
11. **Write the file** to `./rationale/<id>.json` (create the folder), in the shape below.
12. **Check until clean.** Run `ddspace rationale check rationale/<id>.json` and fix every error, then run it
   again, until `ok` is `true`. If `ddspace` is not installed, check the file yourself against the shape below
   (every required field, every point a step names in `points`, every trade-off's values in its step's point) and
   say in your summary that `ddspace` did not check it. Each problem has a `code`, a JSON Pointer `path` into the file and often
   `candidates`: read them, they list what would fit.
13. **Present the explanation.** Summarize the ADRs and the regions that justify the solution, which values are
   forced and which free, the path you took, and the gaps: uncovered decisions, unaddressed requirements and
   approximate fits. In interactive use (not in a study session), when `ddspace` is installed, finish with
   `ddspace rationale build rationale/<id>.json --out .`, which renders the file as a browsable explainer page
   with the trajectory, in `explainers/<id>.html` (with a space, the reference gives the command that renders it
   into the wiki instead).

## Rules

- Every value in the file is an id: of the space, or, without one, a `Dimension: Value` name. Do not name a
  dimension, value or relation in prose that
  the file does not reference.
- Requirement ids in `assessment` and `drivers` must be ids of `problem.requirements`.
- Do not change the file after the last clean check without checking it again.

## File shape

Without a space (with one, the reference gives the `space` part, the ids and the `uncovered` decisions):

```json
{
  "schema": "ddspace-rationale/6",
  "id": "<id>",
  "mode": "explain",
  "space": null,
  "problem": {
    "title": "<problem title>",
    "context": "<problem context>",
    "requirements": [
      {"id": "R1", "kind": "functional", "text": "<requirement>"},
      {"id": "Q1", "kind": "quality", "attribute": "<quality attribute>", "text": "<requirement>"}
    ]
  },
  "budget": {"max_points": {{ default_points }}},
  "points": [
    {"id": "P1", "values": ["<dimension 1>: <value 1.1>", "<dimension 2>: <value 2.1>"], "description": "<what ddspace point --no-space printed, ending with Trade-off: …>", "attested_by": []},
    {"id": "P2", "values": ["<dimension 1>: <value 1.1>"], "description": "<…>", "attested_by": []},
    {"id": "P3", "values": ["<dimension 1>: <value 1.2>"], "description": "<…>", "attested_by": []}
  ],
  "trajectory": [
    {"step": 1, "move": "solution", "parent": null, "values": ["<dimension 1>: <value 1.1>", "<dimension 2>: <value 2.1>"],
     "hypothesis": "the solution as one design point", "assessment": {"R1": "meets", "Q1": "meets"}, "tradeoffs": [],
     "observation": "<what the evaluation showed>", "verdict": "partial", "point": "P1"},
    {"step": 2, "move": "region", "parent": 1, "values": ["<dimension 1>: <value 1.1>"],
     "hypothesis": "<which requirements explain these values>", "assessment": {"Q1": "meets"}, "tradeoffs": [],
     "observation": "<…>", "verdict": "grounded", "point": "P2"},
    {"step": 3, "move": "contrast", "parent": 2, "values": ["<dimension 1>: <value 1.2>"],
     "hypothesis": "<does the requirement force v:1.1?>", "assessment": {"Q1": "misses"}, "tradeoffs": [],
     "observation": "<…: forced>", "verdict": "ungrounded", "point": "P3"}
  ],
  "regions": [
    {"id": "G1", "name": "<short name>", "step": 2, "drivers": ["Q1"], "values": ["<dimension 1>: <value 1.1>"],
     "reason": "<why this region justifies the solution>"}
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "id": "S",
      "name": "<the solution's name>",
      "summary": "<one or two sentences>",
      "step": 1,
      "choices": [
        {"dimension_id": "<dimension 1>", "value_id": "<dimension 1>: <value 1.1>", "drivers": ["Q1"], "forced": true,
         "reason": "<why>", "solution_quote": "<the solution's own words>"},
        {"dimension_id": "<dimension 2>", "value_id": "<dimension 2>: <value 2.1>", "drivers": ["R1"], "forced": null,
         "reason": "<why>", "solution_quote": "<the solution's own words>"}
      ],
      "considered": [],
      "open": [],
      "relations": []
    }
  ],
  "adrs": [
    {"id": "ADR1", "title": "<the decision>", "drivers": ["Q1"], "context": "<what the drivers demand>",
     "options": [
       {"id": "O1", "name": "<the solution's option>", "values": ["<dimension 1>: <value 1.1>"], "status": "chosen", "in": ["S"], "step": 2,
        "pros": ["<…>"], "cons": ["<…>"], "reason": "<forced or free, and why>"},
       {"id": "O2", "name": "<a contrast>", "values": ["<dimension 1>: <value 1.2>"], "status": "discarded", "step": 3,
        "pros": ["<…>"], "cons": ["<…>"], "reason": "<why not>"}
     ]},
    {"id": "ADR2", "title": "<the decision>", "drivers": ["R1"], "context": "<…>",
     "options": [
       {"id": "O1", "name": "<the solution's option>", "values": ["<dimension 2>: <value 2.1>"], "status": "chosen", "in": ["S"],
        "pros": ["<…>"], "cons": ["<…>"], "reason": "<…>"}
     ]}
  ],
  "uncovered": [],
  "unaddressed": []
}
```
